@c ====================================================================
@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex Vulkan
Vulkan is the cross-platform API for GPU graphics and compute.
Guile-Vulkan makes all of it available to Guile programs: every type,
constant and function of Vulkan @value{VULKAN}, including the
window-system extensions for X11 (Xlib and XCB), Wayland and Windows.

The bindings need nothing beyond Guile 3 and a Vulkan loader: no C
compiler and no other Guile libraries.  They are generated from the
Vulkan headers, and follow the same names and rules as
@uref{https://github.com/ebenali/chez-vulkan, chez-vulkan}, the bindings
for Chez Scheme, so code ports easily between the two.

On top of the bindings, @code{(vulkan compute)} provides GPU tensors:
1-D and 2-D arrays of 32-bit floats in GPU memory, with the operations a
small neural network needs (@pxref{GPU Tensors}).

Some notable properties:

@itemize
@item
Structs are bytevectors, and field access compiles to a direct
bytevector access at a constant offset (@pxref{Structs}).

@item
Pointer fields and pointer arguments accept bytevectors, and keep them
alive while Vulkan may use them, so nested structures can be built in
one expression (@pxref{Pointers and Lifetimes}).

@item
Every function is available: those every Vulkan loader exports are bound
directly, and every function can also be fetched at run time
(@pxref{Functions}).
@end itemize

@c ====================================================================
@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@cindex requirements
Guile-Vulkan needs Guile 3.0 and a Vulkan loader (@file{libvulkan.so.1}
on GNU/Linux, @file{vulkan-1.dll} on Windows, @file{libvulkan.1.dylib}
on macOS), plus a Vulkan driver for the device you want to use.  The
window-system examples also need the corresponding client library
(libX11 or libwayland-client).

@cindex Guix
@section With Guix

A Guix package definition for the source tree is in @file{guix.scm}:

@example
guix build -f guix.scm
guix shell -f guix.scm -- guile -c '(use-modules (vulkan))'
@end example

The package finds the Vulkan loader through its store path.  Vulkan
drivers are found as usual, for example Mesa's through
@env{VK_DRIVER_FILES} or @env{XDG_DATA_DIRS}.

@section From a checkout

The modules can be used straight from the source tree by putting it on
Guile's load path:

@example
guile -L /path/to/guile-vulkan
@end example

or by setting @env{GUILE_LOAD_PATH}.  The first time @code{(vulkan)} is
loaded, Guile compiles it, which takes about 20 seconds; the compiled
code is cached, and loading it afterwards takes milliseconds.

