@node Modules
@section Modules

@table @code
@item (vulkan)
Every Vulkan @value{VULKAN} type, constant and function, with the struct
and pointer helpers of @ref{Structs} and @ref{Pointers and Lifetimes}.

@item (vulkan constants)
The constants Vulkan defines with @code{#define}, which the generator
cannot see (@pxref{Constants}).

@item (vulkan xlib)
@itemx (vulkan xcb)
@itemx (vulkan wayland)
@itemx (vulkan win32)
The window-system extensions (@pxref{Window Systems}).

@item (vulkan compute)
GPU tensors (@pxref{GPU Tensors}).

@item (vulkan runtime)
The machinery behind the generated modules.  Its user-facing procedures
are re-exported by @code{(vulkan)}; @code{define-vk-struct} is also
useful for describing C structs of other libraries.
@end table

@node Names
@section Names

@cindex names
Vulkan names become lower-case Scheme names with a @code{vk:} prefix,
words separated by dashes:

@multitable {@code{VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_INSTANCE_CREATE_INFO}} {@code{vk:structure-type-instance-create-info}}
@headitem Vulkan @tab Scheme
@item @code{vkCreateInstance} @tab @code{vk:create-instance}
@item @code{VkInstanceCreateInfo} @tab @code{vk:instance-create-info}
@item @code{VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_INSTANCE_CREATE_INFO} @tab @code{vk:structure-type-instance-create-info}
@item @code{pApplicationInfo} @tab @code{p-application-info}
@end multitable

In a mixed-case name, a dash goes before every upper-case letter that
does not follow another upper-case letter: @code{VkPhysicalDeviceIDProperties}
becomes @code{vk:physical-device-idproperties}.  Struct fields follow the
same rule without the prefix.

A few structs would get the same name as an enum value, for example
@code{VkPipelineCacheHeaderVersionOne} and
@code{VK_PIPELINE_CACHE_HEADER_VERSION_ONE}; such structs get a
@code{-struct} suffix (@code{vk:pipeline-cache-header-version-one-struct}).

Only @code{vk:} names are exported.  The video codec types
(@code{StdVideo@dots{}}) are used inside Vulkan structs but are not
exported.

@node Structs
@section Structs

@cindex structs
@cindex bytevectors
A Vulkan struct is a bytevector of the struct's size, laid out as in C.
Each struct name, such as @code{vk:application-info}, is a macro that
knows the struct's layout, so field access compiles to a direct
bytevector access at a constant offset.  The layouts come from CastXML's
reading of the Vulkan headers.

Fields are named by a @dfn{path}: a list of field names, where a field
holding a nested struct is followed by a field of that struct, and an
array field is followed by an index (any expression).

@deffn {Scheme Syntax} make-vk type [count]
A zeroed bytevector for one @var{type}, or for @var{count} of them in a
row.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Syntax} vk-new type (field @dots{} value) @dots{}
A zeroed @var{type} with the given fields set:

@lisp
(vk-new vk:application-info
        (s-type vk:structure-type-application-info)
        (p-application-name "my app")
        (api-version vk:api-version-1-3))
@end lisp

A field may be a nested path: @code{(stage module m)} sets the
@code{module} field of the struct in the @code{stage} field.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Syntax} vk-ref type (path @dots{}) bv [index]
@deffnx {Scheme Syntax} vk-set! type (path @dots{}) bv [index] value
Read or set the field named by @var{path} in the struct @var{bv}.  With
@var{index}, @var{bv} holds an array of @var{type} and element
@var{index} is used:

@lisp
(vk-ref vk:physical-device-properties (limits max-image-dimension2-d) props)
(vk-ref vk:physical-device-memory-properties
        (memory-types i property-flags) props)
(vk-ref vk:queue-family-properties (queue-flags) families 2)
@end lisp

Integer and floating-point fields are numbers, pointer and handle fields
integers (setting them is described in @ref{Pointers and Lifetimes}).  C
bitfields are read and set like other fields.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Syntax} vk-sizeof type
The size of @var{type} in bytes.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Syntax} vk-offset type (path @dots{})
The byte offset of a (possibly nested) field, for example to read the
string in a fixed-size @code{char} array:

@lisp
(vk-c-string-ref props (vk-offset vk:physical-device-properties (device-name)))
@end lisp
@end deffn

@cindex @code{define-vk-struct}
@deffn {Scheme Syntax} define-vk-struct name size (field type offset) @dots{}
Define @var{name} as a struct of @var{size} bytes, for use with the
macros above.  The generated modules use it for every Vulkan struct; it
is exported by @code{(vulkan runtime)} for describing C structs of other
libraries, as the examples do for X11 events and Wayland's interface
tables.  @var{type} is one of
@code{(u8)}, @code{(s8)}, @code{(u16)}, @code{(s16)}, @code{(u32)},
@code{(s32)}, @code{(u64)}, @code{(s64)}, @code{(f32)}, @code{(f64)},
@code{(ptr)}, @code{(struct @var{name})}, @code{(array @var{n} @var{type})}
or @code{(bits @var{unit} @var{shift} @var{width} @var{signed?})}.
@end deffn

@node Pointers and Lifetimes
@section Pointers and Lifetimes

@cindex pointers
@cindex garbage collection
Pointers and handles are integers.  Wherever Vulkan takes a pointer, a
bytevector can be given instead, and it is kept alive for you:

@itemize
@item
A pointer field set with @code{vk-set!} or @code{vk-new} accepts a
bytevector, a string (stored as a NUL-terminated UTF-8 copy), @code{#f}
(null) or an address.  A bytevector or string stays alive as long as the
struct holding it, until the field is set again.  This makes nested
@code{vk-new} forms safe:

@lisp
(vk:create-instance
 (vk-new vk:instance-create-info
         (s-type vk:structure-type-instance-create-info)
         (p-application-info
          (vk-new vk:application-info
                  (s-type vk:structure-type-application-info)
                  (api-version vk:api-version-1-3))))
 #f slot)
@end lisp

@item
A pointer or handle argument of a Vulkan function accepts a bytevector
or @code{#f}, and the bytevector stays alive during the call.
@end itemize

An address given as an integer is not tracked: the object it points to
must then be kept reachable while Vulkan may use it.  Guile's collector
does not move objects, so addresses stay valid.

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} vk-address bv
The address of bytevector @var{bv}, as an integer.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} vk-bytevector address size
A bytevector over @var{size} bytes of foreign memory at @var{address},
for example memory mapped with @code{vk:map-memory}.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} vk-c-string string
A bytevector holding @var{string} as NUL-terminated UTF-8.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} vk-c-string-array strings
A bytevector holding a C array of pointers to the @var{strings}, as
@code{ppEnabledExtensionNames} wants.  The strings stay alive as long as
the array does.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} vk-c-string-ref bv [offset]
The NUL-terminated UTF-8 string in @var{bv} starting at @var{offset}.
@end deffn

@node Functions
@section Functions

@cindex functions
@cindex out parameters
Vulkan functions are procedures taking the same arguments as in C, in
the same order.  Results such as @code{VkResult} are integers, to compare
with constants like @code{vk:success}.  Output parameters are
bytevectors the function writes into:

@lisp
(define (create-instance info)
  (let ((slot (make-bytevector 8 0)))
    (unless (= vk:success (vk:create-instance info #f slot))
      (error "vkCreateInstance failed"))
    (bytevector-u64-native-ref slot 0)))
@end lisp

@cindex @code{vk:load-*} procedures
@cindex extensions
The functions every Vulkan loader exports (core 1.0--1.4 and the
window-system functions) are bound directly under their names.  If the
installed loader is older and lacks one, calling it raises an error.

Every function, including those, also has a @code{vk:load-} procedure
that fetches it at run time.  It returns a procedure, or @code{#f} if the
function is not available (for example because its extension was not
enabled):

@lisp
(define draw-mesh-tasks (vk:load-cmd-draw-mesh-tasks-ext device))
(when draw-mesh-tasks
  (draw-mesh-tasks command-buffer x y z))
@end lisp

Device-level functions (whose first parameter is a @code{VkDevice},
@code{VkQueue} or @code{VkCommandBuffer}) take a device; the others take
an instance, or @code{#f} for global functions such as
@code{vkCreateInstance}.  Each call looks the function up again, so load
it once and keep the result.

@node Constants
@section Constants

@cindex constants
The enum values and flags of the Vulkan headers are defined in
@code{(vulkan)}.  The constants Vulkan defines with @code{#define} are in
@code{(vulkan constants)}:

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} vk:make-api-version variant major minor patch
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} vk:api-version-major version
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} vk:api-version-minor version
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} vk:api-version-patch version
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} vk:api-version-variant version
Build an API version number, or take one apart.
@end deffn

It also defines @code{vk:api-version-1-0} through
@code{vk:api-version-1-4}, @code{vk:header-version},
@code{vk:true}, @code{vk:false}, @code{vk:whole-size},
@code{vk:queue-family-ignored}, @code{vk:lod-clamp-none} and the other
@code{VK_@dots{}} constants of the headers.

The ``all ones'' constants, such as @code{VK_WHOLE_SIZE} (@code{~0ULL}),
are given as unsigned numbers (@code{vk:whole-size} is
@code{#xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF}), because Guile's FFI and bytevector setters
reject negative numbers for unsigned types.  The same goes for arguments:
an infinite timeout is @code{#xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF}.

@node Window Systems
@section Window Systems

@cindex surfaces
@cindex window systems
@code{(vulkan xlib)}, @code{(vulkan xcb)}, @code{(vulkan wayland)} and
@code{(vulkan win32)} hold each window system's surface structs and
functions, such as @code{vk:wayland-surface-create-info-khr} and
@code{vk:create-wayland-surface-khr}.  They import @code{(vulkan)} for its
types.

@cindex Vulkan loader
The modules load the Vulkan loader under its name on each platform:
@file{libvulkan.so.1} on GNU/Linux and other Unix systems,
@file{vulkan-1.dll} on Windows, and @file{libvulkan.1.dylib} (falling
back to MoltenVK) on macOS.

Opening the window is up to the program; the examples show how to do it
with only Xlib (@file{examples/x11.scm}) or only libwayland-client
(@file{examples/wayland.scm}).

@c ====================================================================
@node GPU Tensors
@chapter GPU Tensors

@cindex tensors
@cindex @code{(vulkan compute)}
@code{(vulkan compute)} provides tensors: 1-D and 2-D arrays of 32-bit
floats in GPU memory, with the operations a small neural network needs.

@lisp
(use-modules (vulkan compute))

(define x (list->tensor '(1 2 3 4 5 6) '(2 3)))
(define w (list->tensor '(1 0 0 1 1 1) '(3 2)))
(tensor->list (tensor-relu (tensor-matmul x w)))   ; @result{} (4.0 5.0 10.0 11.0)
@end lisp

@menu
* Contexts::                Devices and queues.
* Tensors::                 Making tensors and reading them back.
* Operations::              What can be computed.
* Execution and Memory::    When work runs and memory is freed.
@end menu

@node Contexts
@section Contexts

A @dfn{compute context} is a Vulkan device and queue with the objects
tensors need.  One is created on first use, so most programs never deal
with contexts.

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} make-compute-context [#:device n] [#:max-allocations n]
Create a context on Vulkan device number @var{device}, or by default on
the most capable device with a compute queue: a discrete GPU, then an
integrated one, then a software renderer.  @var{max-allocations} lowers
the device's limit on live memory allocations, for testing.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} default-compute-context
The context used when none is given: the value of
@code{current-compute-context} if set, else one made on first use with
@code{make-compute-context}'s defaults.
@end deffn

@defvr {Scheme Parameter} current-compute-context
When set to a context, new tensors are made on it.
@end defvr

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} compute-context? obj
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} compute-context-device-name context
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} compute-context-allocation-count context
A predicate; the device's name; the number of live memory allocations.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} compute-flush! [context]
Submit everything recorded on @var{context} and wait for it
(@pxref{Execution and Memory}).
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} compute-context-destroy! [context]
Wait for @var{context}'s work and destroy it, freeing all its memory.
Its tensors may not be used afterwards.
@end deffn

@node Tensors
@section Tensors

A tensor's shape is @code{(@var{n})} or @code{(@var{rows} @var{cols})},
stored row by row.

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} make-tensor shape [#:context c] [#:fill x]
A tensor of @var{shape} with every element @var{x} (default 0).
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} f32vector->tensor v [shape] [#:context c]
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} list->tensor numbers [shape] [#:context c]
A tensor holding the 32-bit floats of @var{v} (an f32vector or any
bytevector), or the @var{numbers}, with @var{shape}, by default
@code{(@var{length})}.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor->f32vector tensor
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor->list tensor
The elements of @var{tensor}, read back from the GPU after running the
work it depends on.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor-reshape tensor shape
A tensor with @var{shape} sharing @var{tensor}'s elements.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor? obj
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-shape tensor
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-size tensor
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-context tensor
A predicate, the shape, the number of elements, the context.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor-free! tensor
Release @var{tensor}'s GPU memory once pending work is done, without
waiting for the garbage collector.
@end deffn

@cindex interoperability
Since SRFI-4 f32vectors are bytevectors in Guile, data moves between
tensors and other array libraries (SRFI-4, newra, Guile-CV) without
conversion.

@node Operations
@section Operations

Operations make new tensors on the context of their first argument.

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor-matmul a b [#:transpose-a? t] [#:transpose-b? t] [#:alpha x]
@var{alpha} times op(@var{a}) times op(@var{b}), where op transposes its
argument when asked.  op(@var{a}) is an @var{m}-by-@var{k} matrix and
op(@var{b}) a @var{k}-by-@var{n} one; the result is @var{m}-by-@var{n}.  The transposes give the products
backpropagation needs, such as @math{A^T B}, without copying.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor-add a b
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-sub a b
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-mul a b
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-div a b
Elementwise arithmetic.  @var{b} has @var{a}'s size, or is a 1-D tensor
of @var{a}'s column count, applied to every row (for example a bias).
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor-relu a
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-relu-backward x gradient
@code{max(0, @var{a})}; and @var{gradient} where @var{x} is positive,
else 0: the gradient through a ReLU at @var{x}.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor-scale a s
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-add-scalar a s
@var{a} times, or plus, the number @var{s}.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor-exp a
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-tanh a
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-sigmoid a
@deffnx {Scheme Procedure} tensor-copy a
Elementwise functions.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor-softmax-rows a
The softmax of each row of @var{a}.
@end deffn

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} tensor-sum-rows a
The sum of @var{a}'s rows: a 1-D tensor of its column sums (for example
a bias gradient).
@end deffn

@node Execution and Memory
@section Execution and Memory

@cindex batching
Operations are recorded into a command buffer rather than run one by
one.  Reading a tensor back, or calling @code{compute-flush!}, submits
everything recorded and waits for it.  A chain of operations therefore
costs one round trip to the GPU.

@cindex memory
@cindex garbage collection
Tensors are freed by the garbage collector, or with
@code{tensor-free!}, once the GPU is done with them.  Their buffers are
kept and reused for new tensors of the same size, up to 256@tie{}MiB, so
a training loop does not allocate GPU memory every step.

Guile's collector does not see GPU memory, so the module forces a
collection to free unreferenced tensors in three cases: after every
256@tie{}MiB of new tensors; when the number of live allocations nears
the device's limit (which can be as low as 4096); and when an allocation
fails for lack of memory or of allocations, after which it retries once.
For large tensors, @code{tensor-free!} releases memory sooner.

The shaders are in @file{shaders/*.comp}.  @code{guile shaders/build.scm}
compiles them (with glslangValidator) and embeds the SPIR-V in
@code{(vulkan compute shaders)}, so the module needs no files besides
Scheme modules.

